22:07:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve hit on another genuinely powerful medical tool, and you’re absolutely right to point it out. Plasma exchange (plasmapheresis) absolutely saves lives in sepsis and autoimmune diseases, and it does it by removing “bad” plasma. So your point is fair: here is a case where swapping out a component of blood *is* a critical part of treatment. But if we look under the hood, we see that it functions exactly the same as for malaria—it’s a temporary, life‑saving bridge, not a standalone cure. The difference is again about **who’s making the “dirt,” and whether the body can stop making it once the exchange is over.** --- ### How plasma exchange works (and what it doesn’t do) Plasma is the fluid part of blood after you take out red cells, white cells, and platelets. In autoimmune disease, the plasma may be loaded with **harmful antibodies** that the immune system is producing against the body’s own tissues. In sepsis, the plasma is flooded with “cytokine storms”—massive inflammatory signals that cause organ failure. Plasma exchange physically removes those antibodies or cytokines. That can bring a patient back from the brink. The key phrase in your article is likely “life‑saving” because it reverses the immediate crisis. --- ### The hidden caveat: the factory is still running Now ask: **after you’ve beautifully cleaned the plasma, does the patient stay cured?** - **Autoimmune diseases:** The harmful antibodies were made by **B cells and plasma cells** living in the bone marrow, lymph nodes, and spleen. Plasma exchange removes the bullets, but the gun-makers are all still there. Within days to weeks, those cells will pump out new harmful antibodies. That’s why plasma exchange is never given alone. It is always combined with heavy immunosuppressive drugs (corticosteroids, cyclophosphamide, rituximab) or newer biologic agents that directly shut down or kill the antibody-producing cells. The exchange just buys time for those drugs to work. In myast