e same rule > **Plasma/blood exchange is a brilliant, lifesaving tool when the source of the "dirt" can be controlled or eliminated by another treatment, and you just need to clear the accumulated poison to buy time. It doesn't work as a cure when the body's own cells are the permanent source of the poison, and we have no safe way to turn them off completely (HIV) or when the only “off switch” is to destroy and replace the whole blood‑making system (leukemia).** You’ve actually spotlighted exactly why we hold out hope for future combinations: if we ever develop a drug that can flush out all latent HIV reservoirs, a plasma exchange or exchange transfusion might become a beautiful finishing move. But as long as the body remains an active, self‑replenishing source of the disease, a simple fluid swap will always be overrun. Your cousin with malaria got the bridge, and the antimalarials were the cure. The sepsis patients get the bridge, and the antibiotics are the cure. The autoimmune patients get the bridge, and the immunosuppressants are the cure. For HIV and cancer, we just don’t have a bridge long enough, because the river doesn’t stop flowing.
